window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-06-1", "source": "2021年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part , you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay titled“Are people becoming addicted to technology?”.The statement given below is for your reference. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.\n\nNumerous studies claim that addiction to technology is real and it has the same effect on the brain as drug addiction.", "sample": "Are people becoming addicted to technology? My answer is a definite yes. As numerous studies claim that\naddiction to technology is real, this problem deserves our serious attention.\nSigns of such addiction are everywhere in daily life. Many people check their smartphones the moment they\nwake up and keep scrolling short videos until midnight. While technology is designed to make life easier, an\nincreasing number of us feel anxious once we are separated from our screens. As a result, real conversations,\nsleep and study are all disturbed, which shows that our reliance has quietly turned into dependence.\nIn my view, therefore, people are indeed getting hooked on technology. However, this does not mean we\nshould abandon it. What matters is to use technology in a rational way, setting limits on screen time and\nleaving room for the real world."}, "translation": {"prompt": "铁观音(Tieguanyin)是中国最受欢迎的茶之一，原产于福建省安溪县西坪镇，如今安溪全县普遍种植，但该县不同地区生产的铁观音又各具风味。铁观音一年四季均可采摘，尤以春秋两季采摘的茶叶品质最佳。铁观音的加工非常复杂，需要专门的技术和丰富的经验。铁观音含有多种维生素，喝起来口感独特。常饮铁观音有助于预防心脏病、降低血压、增强记忆力。", "sample": "Tieguanyin, originating in Xiping Town, Anxi County, Fujian Province, is one of the most popular teas in China, and is now widely cultivated throughout Anxi County, while that produced in different parts of the county has its own distinctive flavor. It can be picked all year round, with those picked in spring and autumn being of the highest quality. The processing of Tieguanyin is highly complex, requiring specialized techniques and extensive experience. It contains various vitamins and has a unique taste. Drinking it regularly helps prevent heart disease, lower blood pressure, and improve memory."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2021-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Most animals seek shade when temperatures in the Sahara Desert soar to 120 degrees Fahrenheit. But for the Saharan silver ant,  __26__  from their underground nests into the sun's brutal rays to  __27__  for food, this is the perfect time to seek lunch. In 2015 these ants were joined in the desert by scientists from two Belgian universities, who spent a month in the  __28__  heat tracking the ants and digging out their nests. The goal was simple, to discover how the  __29__  adapted to the kind of heat that can  __30__  melt the bottom of shoes.\nBack in Belgium, the scientists looked at the ants under an electronic microscope and found that their  __31__  , triangular hair reflects light like a prism(棱镜), giving them a metallic reflection and protecting them from the sun's awful heat. When Ph.D. student Quentin Willot  __32__  the hair from an ant with a  __33__  knife and put it under a heat lamp, its temperature jumped.\nThe ants' method of staying cool is  __34__  among animals. Could this reflective type of hair protect people? Willot says companies are interested in  __35__  these ants' method of heat protection for human use, including everything from helping to protect the lives of firefighters to keeping homes cool in summer.", "matchIntro": "The start of high school doesn't have to be stressful", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "This month, more than 4 million students across the nation will begin high school. Many will do well. But many will not. Consider that nearly two-thirds of students will experience the \"ninth-grade shock,\" which refers to a dramatic drop in a student's academic performance. Some students cope with this shock by avoiding challenges. For instance, they may drop difficult coursework. Others may experience a hopelessness that results in failing their core classes, such as English, science and math."}, {"label": "B", "text": "This should matter a great deal to parents, teachers and policymakers. Ultimately it should matter to the students themselves and society at large, because students' experience of transitioning(过渡) to the ninth grade can have long-term consequences not only for the students themselves but for their home communities. We make these observations as research psychologists who have studied how schools and families can help young people thrive."}, {"label": "C", "text": "In the new global economy, students who fail to finish the ninth grade with passing grades in college preparatory coursework are very unlikely to graduate on time and go on to get jobs. One study has calculated that the lifetime benefit to the local economy for a single additional student who completes high school is half a million dollars or more. This is based on higher earnings and avoided costs in health care, crime, welfare dependence and other things."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The consequences of doing poorly in the ninth grade can impact more than students' ability to find a good job. It can also impact the extent to which they enjoy life. Students lose many of the friends they turned to for support when they move from the eighth to the ninth grade. One study of ninth-grade students found that 50 percent of friendships among ninth graders changed from one month to the next, signaling striking instability in friendships."}, {"label": "E", "text": "In addition, studies find the first year of high school typically shows one of the greatest increases in depression of any year over the lifespan. Researchers think that one explanation is that ties to friends are broken while academic demands are rising. Furthermore, most adult cases of clinical depression first emerge in adolescence(青春期). The World Health Organization reports that depression has the greatest burden of disease worldwide, in terms of the total cost of treatment and the loss of productivity."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Given all that's riding on having a successful ninth grade experience, it pays to explore what can be done to meet the academic, social and emotional challenges of the transition to high school. So far, our studies have yielded one main insight: Students' beliefs about change—their beliefs about whether people are stuck one way forever, or whether people can change their personalities and abilities—are related to their ability to cope, succeed academically and maintain good mental health. Past research has called these beliefs \"mindsets(思维模式),\" with a \"fixed mindset\" referring to the belief that people cannot change and a \"growth mindset\" referring to the belief that people can change."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In one recent study, we examined 360 adolescents' beliefs about the nature of \"smartness\"—that is, their fixed mindsets about intelligence. We then assessed biological stress responses for students whose grades were dropping by examining their stress hormones(荷尔蒙). Students who believed that intelligence is fixed—that you are stuck being \"not smart\" if you struggle in school—showed higher levels of stress hormones when their grades were declining at the beginning of the ninth grade. If students believed that intelligence could improve—that is to say, when they held more of a growth mindset of intelligence—they showed lower levels of stress hormones when their grades were declining. This was an exciting result because it showed that the body's stress responses are not determined solely by one's grades. Instead, declining grades only predicted worse stress hormones among students who believed that worsening grades were a permanent and hopeless state of affairs."}, {"label": "H", "text": "We also investigated the social side of the high school transition. In this study, instead of teaching students that their smartness can change, we taught them that their social standing—that is, whether they are bullied or excluded or left out—can change over time. We then looked at high school students' stress responses to daily social difficulties. That is, we taught them a growth mindset about their social lives. In this study, students came into the laboratory and were asked to give a public speech in front of upper-year students. The topic of the speech was what makes one popular in high school. Following this, students had to complete a difficult mental math task in front of the same upper-year students."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Experiment results showed that students who were not taught that people can change showed poor stress responses. When these students gave the speech, their blood vessels contracted and their hearts pumped less blood through the body—both responses that the body shows when it is preparing for damage or defeat after a physical threat. Then they gave worse speeches and made more mistakes in math. But when students were taught that people can change, they had better responses to stress, in part because they felt like they had the resources to deal with the demanding situation. Students who got the growth mindset intervention(干预) showed less-contracted blood vessels and their hearts pumped more blood—both of which contributed to more oxygen getting to the brain, and, ultimately, better performance on the speech and mental math tasks."}, {"label": "J", "text": "These findings lead to several possibilities that we are investigating further. First, we are working to replicate(复制) these findings in more diverse school communities. We want to know in which types of schools and for which kinds of students these growth mindset ideas help young people adapt to the challenges of high school. We also hope to learn how teachers, parents or school counselors can help students keep their ongoing academic or social difficulties in perspective. We wonder what would happen if schools helped to make beliefs about the potential for change and improvement a larger feature of the overall school culture, especially for students starting the ninth grade."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Educators and business leaders have more in common than it may seem. Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future. Technology companies have an interest in developing a workforce with the STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) skills needed to grow the company and advance the industry. How can they work together to achieve these goals? Play may be the answer.\nFocusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities. This combination is proving to be the best way to prepare today's children to be the makers and builders of tomorrow. That is why technology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good, old-fashioned play.\nIn fact many experts argue that the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific technologies or subject matter, but to creativity; skills like imagination, problem-finding and problem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment and take risks. These are skills acquired when kids tinker(鼓捣小玩意). High-tech industries such as NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory have found that their best overall problem solvers were master tinkerers in their youth.\nThere are cognitive(认知的) benefits of doing things the way we did as children—building something, tearing it down, then building it up again. Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and five-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical, and architectural activities. This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry.\nIf play and building are critical to 21st-century skill development, that's really good news for two reasons: Children are born builders, makers, and creators, so fostering(培养) 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving kids room to play, tinker and try things out, even as they grow older. Secondly, it doesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills. This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities. Taking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important \"maker\" skills. And anyone, anywhere, can do it.", "47": "Educators and business leaders have more in common than it may seem. Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future. Technology companies have an interest in developing a workforce with the STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) skills needed to grow the company and advance the industry. How can they work together to achieve these goals? Play may be the answer.\nFocusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities. This combination is proving to be the best way to prepare today's children to be the makers and builders of tomorrow. That is why technology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good, old-fashioned play.\nIn fact many experts argue that the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific technologies or subject matter, but to creativity; skills like imagination, problem-finding and problem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment and take risks. These are skills acquired when kids tinker(鼓捣小玩意). High-tech industries such as NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory have found that their best overall problem solvers were master tinkerers in their youth.\nThere are cognitive(认知的) benefits of doing things the way we did as children—building something, tearing it down, then building it up again. Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and five-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical, and architectural activities. This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry.\nIf play and building are critical to 21st-century skill development, that's really good news for two reasons: Children are born builders, makers, and creators, so fostering(培养) 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving kids room to play, tinker and try things out, even as they grow older. Secondly, it doesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills. This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities. Taking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important \"maker\" skills. And anyone, anywhere, can do it.", "48": "Educators and business leaders have more in common than it may seem. Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future. Technology companies have an interest in developing a workforce with the STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) skills needed to grow the company and advance the industry. How can they work together to achieve these goals? Play may be the answer.\nFocusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities. This combination is proving to be the best way to prepare today's children to be the makers and builders of tomorrow. That is why technology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good, old-fashioned play.\nIn fact many experts argue that the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific technologies or subject matter, but to creativity; skills like imagination, problem-finding and problem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment and take risks. These are skills acquired when kids tinker(鼓捣小玩意). High-tech industries such as NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory have found that their best overall problem solvers were master tinkerers in their youth.\nThere are cognitive(认知的) benefits of doing things the way we did as children—building something, tearing it down, then building it up again. Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and five-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical, and architectural activities. This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry.\nIf play and building are critical to 21st-century skill development, that's really good news for two reasons: Children are born builders, makers, and creators, so fostering(培养) 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving kids room to play, tinker and try things out, even as they grow older. Secondly, it doesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills. This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities. Taking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important \"maker\" skills. And anyone, anywhere, can do it.", "49": "Educators and business leaders have more in common than it may seem. Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future. Technology companies have an interest in developing a workforce with the STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) skills needed to grow the company and advance the industry. How can they work together to achieve these goals? Play may be the answer.\nFocusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities. This combination is proving to be the best way to prepare today's children to be the makers and builders of tomorrow. That is why technology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good, old-fashioned play.\nIn fact many experts argue that the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific technologies or subject matter, but to creativity; skills like imagination, problem-finding and problem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment and take risks. These are skills acquired when kids tinker(鼓捣小玩意). High-tech industries such as NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory have found that their best overall problem solvers were master tinkerers in their youth.\nThere are cognitive(认知的) benefits of doing things the way we did as children—building something, tearing it down, then building it up again. Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and five-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical, and architectural activities. This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry.\nIf play and building are critical to 21st-century skill development, that's really good news for two reasons: Children are born builders, makers, and creators, so fostering(培养) 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving kids room to play, tinker and try things out, even as they grow older. Secondly, it doesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills. This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities. Taking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important \"maker\" skills. And anyone, anywhere, can do it.", "50": "Educators and business leaders have more in common than it may seem. Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future. Technology companies have an interest in developing a workforce with the STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) skills needed to grow the company and advance the industry. How can they work together to achieve these goals? Play may be the answer.\nFocusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities. This combination is proving to be the best way to prepare today's children to be the makers and builders of tomorrow. That is why technology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good, old-fashioned play.\nIn fact many experts argue that the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific technologies or subject matter, but to creativity; skills like imagination, problem-finding and problem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment and take risks. These are skills acquired when kids tinker(鼓捣小玩意). High-tech industries such as NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory have found that their best overall problem solvers were master tinkerers in their youth.\nThere are cognitive(认知的) benefits of doing things the way we did as children—building something, tearing it down, then building it up again. Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and five-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical, and architectural activities. This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry.\nIf play and building are critical to 21st-century skill development, that's really good news for two reasons: Children are born builders, makers, and creators, so fostering(培养) 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving kids room to play, tinker and try things out, even as they grow older. Secondly, it doesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills. This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities. Taking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important \"maker\" skills. And anyone, anywhere, can do it.", "51": "Being an information technology, or IT, worker is not a job I envy. They are the ones who, right in the middle of a critical meeting, are expected to instantly fix the projector that's no longer working. They have to tolerate the bad tempers of colleagues frustrated at the number of times they've had to call the help desk for the same issue. They are also the ones who know there are systems that are more powerful, reliable and faster, but their employer simply will not put up the funds to buy them.\nAccording to a recent survey, employees who have a job reliant on IT support consider IT a major source of job dissatisfaction. Through no fault of their own, they can suddenly find their productivity deteriorating or quality control non-existent. And there's little they can do about it.\nThe experience of using IT penetrates almost the entire work field. It has become a crucial part of employees' overall work experience. When IT is operating as it should, employee self-confidence swells. Their job satisfaction, too, can surge when well-functioning machines relieve them of dull tasks or repetitive processes. But if there's one thing that triggers widespread employee frustration, it's an IT transformation project gone wrong, where swollen expectations have been popped and a long list of promised efficiencies has been reversed. This occurs when business leaders implement IT initiatives with little consideration of how those changes will impact the end user.\nWhich is why managers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to their employees, and exert substantial effort in ensuring their IT team eliminates programming errors and application crashes. Adequate and timely IT support should also be available to enable users to cope with technological issues at work. More importantly, IT practitioners need to understand what employees experience mentally when they use IT.\nTherefore, businesses need to set up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit in with their employees' work, rather than adjust their work to fit in with the company's IT limitations.", "52": "Being an information technology, or IT, worker is not a job I envy. They are the ones who, right in the middle of a critical meeting, are expected to instantly fix the projector that's no longer working. They have to tolerate the bad tempers of colleagues frustrated at the number of times they've had to call the help desk for the same issue. They are also the ones who know there are systems that are more powerful, reliable and faster, but their employer simply will not put up the funds to buy them.\nAccording to a recent survey, employees who have a job reliant on IT support consider IT a major source of job dissatisfaction. Through no fault of their own, they can suddenly find their productivity deteriorating or quality control non-existent. And there's little they can do about it.\nThe experience of using IT penetrates almost the entire work field. It has become a crucial part of employees' overall work experience. When IT is operating as it should, employee self-confidence swells. Their job satisfaction, too, can surge when well-functioning machines relieve them of dull tasks or repetitive processes. But if there's one thing that triggers widespread employee frustration, it's an IT transformation project gone wrong, where swollen expectations have been popped and a long list of promised efficiencies has been reversed. This occurs when business leaders implement IT initiatives with little consideration of how those changes will impact the end user.\nWhich is why managers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to their employees, and exert substantial effort in ensuring their IT team eliminates programming errors and application crashes. Adequate and timely IT support should also be available to enable users to cope with technological issues at work. More importantly, IT practitioners need to understand what employees experience mentally when they use IT.\nTherefore, businesses need to set up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit in with their employees' work, rather than adjust their work to fit in with the company's IT limitations.", "53": "Being an information technology, or IT, worker is not a job I envy. They are the ones who, right in the middle of a critical meeting, are expected to instantly fix the projector that's no longer working. They have to tolerate the bad tempers of colleagues frustrated at the number of times they've had to call the help desk for the same issue. They are also the ones who know there are systems that are more powerful, reliable and faster, but their employer simply will not put up the funds to buy them.\nAccording to a recent survey, employees who have a job reliant on IT support consider IT a major source of job dissatisfaction. Through no fault of their own, they can suddenly find their productivity deteriorating or quality control non-existent. And there's little they can do about it.\nThe experience of using IT penetrates almost the entire work field. It has become a crucial part of employees' overall work experience. When IT is operating as it should, employee self-confidence swells. Their job satisfaction, too, can surge when well-functioning machines relieve them of dull tasks or repetitive processes. But if there's one thing that triggers widespread employee frustration, it's an IT transformation project gone wrong, where swollen expectations have been popped and a long list of promised efficiencies has been reversed. This occurs when business leaders implement IT initiatives with little consideration of how those changes will impact the end user.\nWhich is why managers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to their employees, and exert substantial effort in ensuring their IT team eliminates programming errors and application crashes. Adequate and timely IT support should also be available to enable users to cope with technological issues at work. More importantly, IT practitioners need to understand what employees experience mentally when they use IT.\nTherefore, businesses need to set up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit in with their employees' work, rather than adjust their work to fit in with the company's IT limitations.", "54": "Being an information technology, or IT, worker is not a job I envy. They are the ones who, right in the middle of a critical meeting, are expected to instantly fix the projector that's no longer working. They have to tolerate the bad tempers of colleagues frustrated at the number of times they've had to call the help desk for the same issue. They are also the ones who know there are systems that are more powerful, reliable and faster, but their employer simply will not put up the funds to buy them.\nAccording to a recent survey, employees who have a job reliant on IT support consider IT a major source of job dissatisfaction. Through no fault of their own, they can suddenly find their productivity deteriorating or quality control non-existent. And there's little they can do about it.\nThe experience of using IT penetrates almost the entire work field. It has become a crucial part of employees' overall work experience. When IT is operating as it should, employee self-confidence swells. Their job satisfaction, too, can surge when well-functioning machines relieve them of dull tasks or repetitive processes. But if there's one thing that triggers widespread employee frustration, it's an IT transformation project gone wrong, where swollen expectations have been popped and a long list of promised efficiencies has been reversed. This occurs when business leaders implement IT initiatives with little consideration of how those changes will impact the end user.\nWhich is why managers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to their employees, and exert substantial effort in ensuring their IT team eliminates programming errors and application crashes. Adequate and timely IT support should also be available to enable users to cope with technological issues at work. More importantly, IT practitioners need to understand what employees experience mentally when they use IT.\nTherefore, businesses need to set up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit in with their employees' work, rather than adjust their work to fit in with the company's IT limitations.", "55": "Being an information technology, or IT, worker is not a job I envy. They are the ones who, right in the middle of a critical meeting, are expected to instantly fix the projector that's no longer working. They have to tolerate the bad tempers of colleagues frustrated at the number of times they've had to call the help desk for the same issue. They are also the ones who know there are systems that are more powerful, reliable and faster, but their employer simply will not put up the funds to buy them.\nAccording to a recent survey, employees who have a job reliant on IT support consider IT a major source of job dissatisfaction. Through no fault of their own, they can suddenly find their productivity deteriorating or quality control non-existent. And there's little they can do about it.\nThe experience of using IT penetrates almost the entire work field. It has become a crucial part of employees' overall work experience. When IT is operating as it should, employee self-confidence swells. Their job satisfaction, too, can surge when well-functioning machines relieve them of dull tasks or repetitive processes. But if there's one thing that triggers widespread employee frustration, it's an IT transformation project gone wrong, where swollen expectations have been popped and a long list of promised efficiencies has been reversed. This occurs when business leaders implement IT initiatives with little consideration of how those changes will impact the end user.\nWhich is why managers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to their employees, and exert substantial effort in ensuring their IT team eliminates programming errors and application crashes. Adequate and timely IT support should also be available to enable users to cope with technological issues at work. More importantly, IT practitioners need to understand what employees experience mentally when they use IT.\nTherefore, businesses need to set up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit in with their employees' work, rather than adjust their work to fit in with the company's IT limitations."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What did Joe's parents decide to do?", "options": ["A) Enrol him in a Newcastle football club.", "B) Send him to an after-school art class.", "C) Forbid him to draw in his workbook.", "D) Help him post his drawings online."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Send him to an after-school art class.\n【定位】Rather than shutting down the habit of drawing in his school's workbook, Joe's parents\ndecided to encourage his creativity by sending their son to an after-school art class.\n乔的父母没有去扼杀他在校练习册上画画的习惯，反而决定鼓励他的创造力，把儿子送去上\n一个课后美术班。\n【信号】Rather than后的转折落点。答案信号是句首的 Rather than shutting down the habit of\ndrawing——\"而不是去扼杀\"。Rather than 是一个含蓄否定的转折标志：它先抛出一个被\n否定的做法（禁止画画），真正做的事在它后面的 decided to encourage his creativity\nby sending their son to an after-school art class。转折/否定之后才是落点，这是听力定\n位的核心信号。\n【替换】sending their son to an after-school art class ↔ Send him to an after-school art class.\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说\"给他报名 Newcastle 足球俱乐部\"。录音确实念到了 He's great at football. 和地名\nNewcastle，但那是第3段描述乔\"球踢得好\"，第2段 Newcastle 是餐厅所在地，根本没\n有\"父母给他报足球队\"这回事。它把零散原词拼成了假事件。\n借用原文：He's great at football.\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说\"禁止他在练习册上画画\"。录音里 shutting down the habit of drawing in his\nschool's workbook 确实提到\"扼杀在练习册上画画的习惯\"，但它前面有 Rather than——\n是父母\"没有\"做的事。C 恰恰取了被转折否定掉的那半句，方向反了。\n借用原文：shutting down the habit of drawing in his school's workbook\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说\"帮他把画发到网上\"。录音里 His teacher recognized Joe's talent and posted all\nhis work online 确实有\"发到网上\"，但做这件事的是\"老师\"，不是\"父母\"。题问的是父母，\n把老师的行为安到父母头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：His teacher recognized Joe's talent and posted all his work online", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the restaurant \"Number 4\" do?", "options": ["A) Contacted Joe to decorate its dining-room.", "B) Hired Joe to paint all the walls of its buildings.", "C) Renovated its kitchen and all the dining-rooms.", "D) Asked Joe for permission to use his online drawings."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Contacted Joe to decorate its dining-room.\n【定位】A restaurant named \"Number 4\" in Newcastle contacted Joe's teacher to ask if the 9-year-old\ncould come and decorate its dining-room with his drawings.\n一家位于纽卡斯尔、名叫\"Number 4\"的餐厅联系了乔的老师，问这个9岁的孩子能不能来用\n他的画装饰餐厅的用餐区。\n【信号】题目同序顺听定位。新闻题目通常与播报同序：第1题答案在第1段，第2题就顺听到第2段。\n本段开头 A restaurant named \"Number 4\" in Newcastle contacted Joe's teacher 一出\n现餐厅这个主语，就是第2题的答案句。顺着播报次序接题，不用回跳。\n【替换】contacted Joe's teacher to ask if the 9-year-old could come and decorate its dining-room ↔\nContacted Joe to decorate its dining-room.\nconcise概括对应\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nB 说\"雇佣乔粉刷其建筑的所有墙面\"。录音确实有 put his ideas straight on the wall（把\n想法画到墙上），但 B 把它夸成 paint all the walls（所有墙），还说成 Hired（雇佣、含\n付薪），录音只说 contacted...to ask（联系询问），既没说付钱雇佣、也没说所有墙面。\n借用原文：put his ideas straight on the wall\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说\"翻新了厨房和所有的用餐区\"。录音说的是 decorate its dining-room——装饰\"用餐\n区\"，C 一是把 decorate（用画装饰）偷换成 Renovated（整体翻新装修），二是凭空加了\nkitchen（厨房）和 all（所有），范围和动作都被放大，录音都没说。\n借用原文：decorate its dining-room\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说\"请求乔授权使用他网上的画\"。录音里 posted all his work online 确有\"发到网上\"，\n但那是老师发画的动作，餐厅这边只是 contacted Joe's teacher to ask if the 9-year-old\ncould come——请人来现场画，根本没有\"求授权用网上的画\"这回事。online 是被挪用的\n原词。\n借用原文：posted all his work online\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Kirsteen Marshall, a 34-year-old mum of one, posted a tearful video on social media Wednesday,\nbegging for the safe return of her beloved pet dog. After combing through the security video outside a\nGorbals shop, Kirsteen has now posted an image of a man suspected of stealing the dog.\n34岁的单亲妈妈Kirsteen Marshall周三在社交媒体上发布了一段含泪视频,恳求安全归还她心爱的宠物\n狗。在仔细查看Gorbals一家商店外的监控视频后,Kirsteen现已发布了一名涉嫌偷狗男子的照片。\n2 The image appears to show a man carrying the dog in his arms. Kirsteen also believes the video\nobtained from the shop shows the dog being stolen by a man before driving off in a car which had been\nwaiting nearby.\n照片似乎显示一名男子抱着这只狗。Kirsteen还认为从商店获得的视频显示,这只狗被一名男子偷走,随后\n乘坐一辆在附近等候的汽车离开。\n3 The family is now offering a £5,000 reward for the safe return of the dog after launching a social\nmedia campaign to find the thief.\n在发起社交媒体活动寻找窃贼后,这家人现在悬赏5000英镑以安全找回这只狗。\n4 The dog is six and a half years old and was last seen wearing a red collar.\n这只狗六岁半,最后被看到时戴着红色项圈。\n5 Kirsteen said: \"We'll pay that to anyone who brings him home as long as they are not responsible for\nhis disappearance.\"\nKirsteen说:\"只要他们不是对狗的失踪负有责任,我们会付钱给任何把它带回家的人。\n6 Police are investigating the incident.\n警方正在调查此事。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is Kirsteen Marshall trying to do?", "options": ["A) Get her pet dog back.", "B) Identify the suspect on the security video.", "C) Beg for help from the police.", "D) Post pictures of her pet dog on social media."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Get her pet dog back.\n【定位】Kirsteen Marshall, a 34-year-old mum of one, posted a tearful video on social media\nWednesday, begging for the safe return of her beloved pet dog.\n34 岁、育有一子的母亲 Kirsteen Marshall 周三在社交媒体上发布了一段含泪的视频，恳求\n让她心爱的宠物狗平安归来。\n【信号】begging 点出真实目的。这一句里 begging for the safe return of her beloved pet dog\n是表意图的动词短语，直接道出她做这一切的根本目的，是承载答案的信号句；前面\nposted a tearful video 只是她采取的手段。\n【替换】begging for the safe return of her beloved pet dog ↔ Get her pet dog back\n求平安归来=把狗找回\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说她 posted an image of a man suspected of stealing the dog，B 盗用了这里\n的 suspect 原词，但发嫌疑人照片是她找狗路上的一个中间手段，不是她『努力要达成』的\n目的，答非所问。\n借用原文：After combing through the security video outside a Gorbals shop, Kirsteen has now\nposted an image of a man suspected of stealing the dog\nC) 答非所问\n录音末尾只说 Police are investigating the incident.，是警方在查案，并没有说她去『乞\n求警方帮忙』；C 把警方的存在偷换成她的诉求，与题干问的『她想做什么』错位。\n借用原文：Police are investigating the incident.\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说她 posted a tearful video on social media，D 盗用 social media、post 原词，但\n发社交媒体只是她为找回狗采取的途径，跟 B 一样是手段而非她要达成的目的。\n借用原文：posted a tearful video on social media Wednesday", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the news report say about Kirsteen Marshall's family?", "options": ["A) It is suffering a great deal from the incident.", "B) It is helping the police with the investigation.", "C) It is bringing the case to the local district court.", "D) It is offering a big reward to anyone who helps."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is offering a big reward to anyone who helps.\n【定位】The family is now offering a £5,000 reward for the safe return of the dog after launching a\nsocial media campaign to find the thief.\n在发起社交媒体行动寻找窃贼之后，这家人如今正悬赏 5000 英镑以换回狗的平安归来。\n【信号】family+具体金额坐实。这一句以题干主语 The family 起头，并给出具体金额 offering a\n£5,000 reward，主语对上、又带数字，正是承载答案的信号句；预读时圈的 reward 在此\n被坐实。\n【替换】offering a £5,000 reward for the safe return of the dog ↔\noffering a big reward to anyone who helps\n5000镑=一大笔悬赏\nWe'll pay that to anyone who brings him home ↔ to anyone who helps\n带它回家者=帮忙者\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音第一段说她发了 a tearful video（含泪视频），A 据此推出全家『受了很大煎熬』，但\n报道只描述了行动（发视频、悬赏），并未陈述家人正承受痛苦这一事实，属于在原文情绪\n线索上推过了头。\n借用原文：posted a tearful video on social media Wednesday\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音说 Police are investigating the incident.，是警方在调查；B 盗用 police、\ninvestigation 原词，却把『警方查案』安到了家人头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Police are investigating the incident.\nC) 未提及\n全篇只讲发视频、贴嫌疑人照片、悬赏、警方调查，从没说过把案子告上 local district\ncourt；C 是录音里完全没有依据的动作，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does Guy Wilson say his café aims to do?", "options": ["A) Provide free meals to the local poor.", "B) Help people connect with each other.", "C) Help eliminate class difference in his area.", "D) Provide customers with first-class service."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Help people connect with each other.\n【定位】Owner Guy Wilson says his café aims to build community, rather than profits. He wants to\nprovide a bridge for people to connect in an area that has been divided by class and wealth by\nproviding affordable breakfasts.\n店主盖伊·威尔逊说，他的咖啡馆志在营造社区，而非赚取利润。他想在一个被阶层和财富割\n裂的地区，通过提供负担得起的早餐，为人们搭起一座相互连接的桥梁。\n【信号】表目的的 aims to / wants to。这句里 aims to build community和 wants to provide a\nbridge for people to connect接连出现两个表目的的信号词；预读时锁定的就是它们，听\n到\"aims to\"后面那串内容即答案区，无须再往后听。\n【替换】provide a bridge for people to connect ↔ Help people connect with each other.\n搭桥让人连接=帮人互相连接\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n原文说店主靠 providing affordable breakfasts（提供负担得起的早餐）来搭桥，\naffordable 是\"付得起、便宜\"，并不等于 free（免费）；A 把\"affordable\"夸大成 free\nmeals，又添了\"the local poor（当地穷人）\"这个原文没指明的对象，是听到\"早餐\"就误选\n的陷阱。\n借用原文：providing affordable breakfasts\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文只说该地区 has been divided by class and wealth（被阶层和财富所割裂），店是想\n在这种环境里\"搭桥连接\"，从没说要 eliminate（消除）阶层差异。C 盗用了 class 这个原\n词，再把\"搭桥\"夸大成\"消除差异\"——听到 class 就选它正中陷阱。\n借用原文：has been divided by class and wealth\nD) 未提及\n原文明确老板要的是 build community, rather than profits（营造社区而非利润），目标\n根本不在\"服务档次\"上；D 的 first-class service（一流服务）属于无中生有，还隐隐与\"不\n追求利润\"的取向相反，是用一个听起来\"高大上\"的褒义短语来诱选。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the news report say about Eggs & Bread café?", "options": ["A) It does not supervise its employees.", "B) It donates regularly to a local charity.", "C) It does not use volunteers.", "D) It is open round the clock."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It does not use volunteers.\n【定位】The café doesn't use volunteers, but pays its staff to ensure consistency in its service.\n这家咖啡馆不使用志愿者，而是付薪给员工，以确保服务的稳定一致。\n【信号】否定 doesn't + 转折 but。答案句靠 doesn't use volunteers这个明确否定，加上 but\npays its staff的转折点出真意——\"不用义工、改用付薪员工\"。否定词与转折词同现，正是\n答案落点的强信号。\n【替换】doesn't use volunteers ↔ It does not use volunteers.\ndoesn't=does not，几乎原句\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文说店 has two members of staff or supervisors on shift every day（每天有两名员\n工或主管值班），明明是\"有人盯班、管得到\"；A 盗用 supervisors / staff 这层意思却反过\n来说 does not supervise its employees（不监管员工），与原文相反，是听\n到\"supervisor\"就误选的陷阱。\n借用原文：has two members of staff or supervisors on shift every day\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文说店 doesn't take donations and doesn't want to be seen as a charity（不接受捐\n款、也不想被当成慈善机构）；B 盗用 donations / charity 两个原词，却说成 donates\nregularly to a local charity（定期向当地慈善机构捐款），方向完全反了——这是\"听到\ncharity 就选\"的高诱惑陷阱。\n借用原文：doesn't take donations and doesn't want to be seen as a charity\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说店 is open in the mornings every day of the year（一年中每天早上营业）；D 的\nopen round the clock 是\"24 小时全天候\"，把\"每天早上\"夸大成\"全天不打烊\"，是听到\nopen every day 就误选的时间细节陷阱。\n借用原文：is open in the mornings every day of the year", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What happens when people start to know each other according to Guy Wilson?", "options": ["A) They will realise the importance of communication.", "B) They will come to the café even more frequently.", "C) They will care less about their own background.", "D) They will find they have something in common."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They will find they have something in common.\n【定位】Mr. Wilson says, when people start to know other people around them, they realize they're not\nthat different. And whatever their financial background, or their educational background,\nmost people will have something in common with each other.\n威尔逊先生说，当人们开始认识身边的人，就会意识到彼此并没有那么不同。而且无论财务\n背景还是教育背景如何，大多数人彼此之间都会有一些共同之处。\n【信号】条件 when + 结果 they realize / will have。答案句以 when people start to know\nother people around them起头，后面 they realize they're not that different和 most\npeople will have something in common就是结果。题干的 when 与原文的 when 对齐，\n结果就在 when 之后，顺着听即可锁定。\n【替换】most people will have something in common with each other ↔\nThey will find they have something in common.\nhave something in common 原样保留\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文老板谈的是人们会 realize they're not that different（意识到彼此没那么不同），从\n没说他们会意识到 communication（沟通）的重要性；A 的\"沟通\"是原文没有的概念，属\n凭\"咖啡馆=交流\"的常识补出来的项。\nB) 原词陷阱\ncafé 一词录音里反复出现，B 的 come to the café even more frequently（更频繁地来\n店）就借这个原词诱选；但原文谈的是人熟了之后\"意识到彼此相似\"，根本没说人们会更常\n来，属听到 café 就选的答非所问。\n借用原文：when people start to know other people around them\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说 whatever their financial background, or their educational background, most\npeople will have something in common（无论何种背景，大多有共同点）；C 盗用\nbackground 原词，却把结论换成 care less about their own background（更少在意自己\n的背景）——原文讲\"有共同点\"，不是讲\"在意程度变低\"，是 D 的近义干扰项。\n借用原文：whatever their financial background, or their educational background, most people will\nhave something in common", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) A surprise party for Paul's birthday.", "B) Travel plans for the coming weekend.", "C) Preparations for Saturday's get-together.", "D) The new market on the other side of town."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） Preparations for Saturday's get-together.\n【定位】M: So what time do you think we should have the party on Saturday? W: How about inviting\npeople to come at 6:00 p.m.? Then we'll have the afternoon to prepare food and drink, and\nstuff like that.\n男：那你觉得我们周六的聚会该几点开始？女：邀请大家下午六点来怎么样？这样我们就有\n一下午来准备食物和饮料之类的东西。\n【信号】开头点题定主线。对话第一句 what time do you think we should have the party on\nSaturday 一上来就把周六聚会摆出来，紧接着女方说 we'll have the afternoon to\nprepare food and drink，之后几段一直在谈准备食物、买饮料、放音乐——开头点题加上\n全程不离筹备聚会，主线就此锁定。\n【替换】prepare food and drink ↔ Preparations\n动词 prepare 转名词 Preparations\nthe party on Saturday ↔ Saturday's get-together\nparty 与 get-together 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA surprise party for Paul's birthday. 借了录音末尾真出现的人名 Paul，但原文 we\nshould ask Paul to bring his computer and speakers 只是请 Paul 带设备放音乐，从没\n说这是给 Paul 办的生日惊喜会，听到 Paul 就选属于原词陷阱。\n借用原文：ask Paul to bring his computer and speakers\nB) 未提及\nTravel plans for the coming weekend. 借了周末这个时间框，但全程谈的是在家办聚会、\n买东西，没有半句出行旅行，把周末筹备聚会偷换成周末旅行计划，属未提及内容。\nD) 原词陷阱\nThe new market on the other side of town. 确实是录音 that big new supermarket on\nthe other side of town 念到的，但它只是买饮料时顺带提的一个去处，是细节而非全篇主\n线，主旨题选了细节就以偏概全。\n借用原文：that big new supermarket on the other side of town", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Why does the woman say it is a good idea to serve foods that guests can help themselves to?", "options": ["A) It makes the hostess's job a whole lot easier.", "B) It enables guests to walk around and chat freely.", "C) It saves considerable time and labor.", "D) It requires fewer tables and chairs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It enables guests to walk around and chat freely.\n【定位】so that people can just help themselves, rather than getting everyone to sit down at the table to\neat a meal. I think that's a bit too formal. It's better to let people walk around and talk to each\nother, or sit where they like.\n这样大家就能自助取用，而不必让所有人坐到桌边吃一顿正餐。我觉得那样有点太正式了。\n还是让大家走动走动、互相聊聊、想坐哪儿坐哪儿更好。\n【信号】it's better to 引出真正理由。女方先说自助取餐 so that people can just help\nthemselves，再用 It's better to let people walk around and talk to each other 给出她\n认为好的真正原因——it's better to 这个引出理由的信号句，正是答案落点。\n【替换】let people walk around and talk to each other ↔ enables guests to walk around and chat freely\ntalk 换 chat、let 换 enables\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt makes the hostess's job a whole lot easier. 听着合理，但原文女方给的理由是让客人\n能走动交谈，从没说这能让女主人活儿更轻松，属于自己顺势补的推断，原文未提。\nC) 未提及\nIt saves considerable time and labor. 是常识里自助餐的好处，但本题问的是女方说的理\n由，她说的是社交自由不是省时省力，录音并未把节省时间精力当作理由，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt requires fewer tables and chairs. 原文虽提到 sit down at the table，但只是说不必正\n式围桌吃饭，从没说自助能少用桌椅，这是借了 table 一词的推断，原文未作此理由。\n借用原文：getting everyone to sit down at the table", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the woman say about the new supermarket?", "options": ["A) It offers some big discounts.", "B) It is quite close to her house.", "C) It is more spacious and less crowded.", "D) It sells local wines and soft drinks."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It offers some big discounts.\n【定位】And I heard that the new supermarket offers some big discounts to attract customers. So\ngoing there should be a great idea.\n而且我听说这家新超市为了吸引顾客提供很大的折扣，所以去那儿应该是个好主意。\n【信号】女方直接陈述听到的信息。题干问的是 the woman，而正是女方在第 6 段说 I heard that\nthe new supermarket offers some big discounts——说话人正确（女方）加上直接陈述\n新超市的特点，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】the new supermarket offers some big discounts ↔ It offers some big discounts.\n几近原句的精简\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt is quite close to her house. 原文从没说这家新超市离谁家近，反而男方说 the other\nside of town（在城另一头），与近相反，属未提及甚至相反。\nC) 未提及\nIt is more spacious and less crowded. 听着像大超市该有的样子，但录音对新超市只说了\n打折和在城另一头，从没提宽敞或人少，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt sells local wines and soft drinks. 借了录音里真出现的 some beer and wine、other\nsoft drinks，但那是男方说我们要买的饮料清单，不是在描述新超市卖什么，把买什么错安\n成超市卖什么，属原词陷阱兼张冠李戴。\n借用原文：get some beer and wine, and some fruit juice, and other soft drinks", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the man suggest they ask Paul to do?", "options": ["A) Cook a dish for the party.", "B) Arrive 10 minutes earlier.", "C) Prepare a few opening remarks.", "D) Bring his computer and speakers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Bring his computer and speakers.\n【定位】Maybe we should ask Paul to bring his computer and speakers, so that we can play\nsome music.\n也许我们该请 Paul 把他的电脑和音箱带来，这样我们就能放点音乐了。\n【信号】ask…to 引出被建议的动作。男方用提议句 Maybe we should ask Paul to bring his\ncomputer and speakers——Maybe we should ask…to 这个建议信号直接点出请 Paul\n做的事，是答案句。\n【替换】bring his computer and speakers ↔ Bring his computer and speakers.\n近乎原词的动作短语\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nCook a dish for the party. 蹭了前文谈食物 prepare some sandwiches and snacks 的话\n题，但请 Paul 做的事是带设备放音乐，跟做菜无关，属答非所问。\n借用原文：prepare some sandwiches and snacks\nB) 未提及\nArrive 10 minutes earlier. 原文只在第 2 段说客人六点来，从没让 Paul 早到十分钟，数字\n和动作都是凭空添的，属未提及。\nC) 未提及\nPrepare a few opening remarks. 原文从没提让 Paul 致辞或准备开场白，整个对话也没出\n现演讲环节，纯属凭空编造，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why does the woman want to have a car?", "options": ["A) For commuting to work.", "B) For long-distance travel.", "C) For getting around in Miami.", "D) For convenience at weekends."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） For convenience at weekends.\n【定位】I wouldn't need to use it every day, but I think it would be very convenient to have one for\nthe weekends.\n我不用每天都开它，但我觉得周末有辆车会很方便。\n【信号】转折 but 之后落真意。女士先说 I wouldn't need to use it every day（不用每天开），紧\n跟一个转折 but，but 之后 very convenient to have one for the weekends 才是她真正\n想要车的理由。听力里转折词后面那半句几乎总是落点，前半句多是铺垫或干扰。\n【替换】convenient to have one for the weekends ↔ For convenience at weekends.\n形容词转名词、说法基本同形\n【排除】A) 未提及\nFor commuting to work.\"为了上下班通勤\"是常识里买车最常见的理由，听起来很顺，但\n全文女士从没说过上班通勤，反而强调 I wouldn't need to use it every day（不用每天\n开），与通勤正相反。\nB) 未提及\nFor long-distance travel.\"用于长途出行\"原文同样只字未提；女士只说周末用、不必天天\n用，没有任何关于长途旅行的信息。\nC) 原词陷阱\nFor getting around in Miami. 盗用了原文出现过的地名 Miami 和短语\"代步\"，但原文 I\nactually owned a car for a little while when I lived in Miami 说的是\"过去住 Miami\n时\"曾有车，那是往事，不是她现在想买车的理由。听到 Miami 就选，正中圈套。\n借用原文：when I lived in Miami", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the woman say about German cars?", "options": ["A) They are reliable.", "B) They are compact.", "C) They are spacious.", "D) They are easy to drive."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are reliable.\n【定位】I've heard that German cars are very reliable, but I haven't decided on a specific model yet.\n我听说德国车很可靠，但我还没定下具体的车型。\n【信号】钩子词 German cars 后的直接评价。原文 I've heard that German cars are very reliable\n里，钩子词 German cars 一出现，紧跟的形容词 reliable 就是女士给出的评价，正是答案\n句。这里的信号是\"听音钩子词 + 其后的描述词\"。\n【替换】German cars are very reliable ↔ They are reliable.\n原词保留＋代词替名词\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nThey are compact.\"小巧\"挪用了女士后面说的 I'd also like it to be small（希望车小一\n点），但那是她对\"想买的车\"的个人偏好，并不是她\"对德国车的评价\"，把愿望安到了德国\n车头上，问的是德国车特点，答非所问。\n借用原文：I'd also like it to be small\nC) 未提及\nThey are spacious.\"宽敞\"原文完全没出现，且与女士\"想要小车\"的偏好正好相反，是用一\n个和 B 反义的形容词来凑数干扰。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey are easy to drive. 盗用了原文 so that it's easy to drive in the city，但那句是接\n着\"想要小车\"说的——小车在城里好开，讲的是她想买的小车，不是德国车的特点，对象搞\n错了。\n借用原文：so that it's easy to drive in the city", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man recommend the woman do?", "options": ["A) Buy a second-hand car.", "B) Trust her own judgment.", "C) Seek advice from his friend.", "D) Look around before deciding."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Seek advice from his friend.\n【定位】I have a friend who sells second-hand cars. He's a really nice guy, and he knows a lot\nabout cars. I could give you his phone number if you want, and you could call him and ask\nhim questions.\n我有个卖二手车的朋友。他人很好，对车很在行。你愿意的话我可以把他的电话给你，你可\n以打给他问问题。\n【信号】建议句 I could…/you could… 是落点。男士用 I could give you his phone number 和\nyou could call him and ask him questions 这种 could 引出的婉转建议句给出主意——\n找他那位懂车的朋友咨询。建议类题目的答案就落在这种 \"you could… / I could…\" 句上。\n【替换】I have a friend who sells second-hand cars ↔ Seek advice from his friend\nhis friend 原义保留\nyou could call him and ask him questions ↔ Seek advice from his friend\n详述动作概括为求教\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBuy a second-hand car. 盗用了原文 a friend who sells second-hand cars 里的 second-\nhand cars，但那只是介绍朋友\"卖二手车\"的身份，男士建议的是\"去问他\"，不是\"去买二手\n车\"；女士还明确说 I don't want to feel obliged to buy one of his cars（不想被逼着买\n车）。听到 second-hand 就选即落陷阱。\n借用原文：a friend who sells second-hand cars\nB) 与原文相反\nTrust her own judgment.\"相信自己的判断\"恰好和原文相反：女士最后说 I could use\nsome help in deciding、Speaking to an expert would be a good idea（需要帮助、找\n行家聊聊好），说明她接受了\"求助\"而非\"靠自己判断\"，更不是男士的建议。\n借用原文：I could use some help in deciding\nD) 未提及\nLook around before deciding.\"先多看看再决定\"是买东西的常识做法，听着合理，但男士\n的建议自始至终是\"找他朋友问\"，从没说过\"先到处比较\"，属脑补出来的干扰。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's friend from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He sells new cars.", "B) He can be trusted.", "C) He is starting a business.", "D) He is a successful car dealer."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He can be trusted.\n【定位】Oh, no, he's not like that. He's a good friend of mine, and he would never try to pressure you\nor cheat you.\n哦，不，他不是那种人。他是我的好朋友，绝不会给你压力或欺骗你。\n【信号】对人品的强调：never pressure or cheat。男士强调 he would never try to pressure\nyou or cheat you（绝不施压、绝不欺骗），加上女士随后说 if you trust him, then, I\nguess it should be OK，两处合起来把重点落在\"这位朋友可信\"上。信号是\"对人品的反复\n强调 + 对方表示信任\"。\n【替换】he would never try to pressure you or cheat you ↔ He can be trusted\n否定式具体描述＝肯定式概括评价\nif you trust him, then, I guess it should be OK ↔ He can be trusted\ntrust 原词印证可信\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nHe sells new cars. 借用了原文\"卖车\"的信息，却把车型说反了：原文是 a friend who sells\nsecond-hand cars（卖二手车），不是新车。听到\"sells…cars\"就选，会被 new/second-\nhand 的差别绊倒。\n借用原文：a friend who sells second-hand cars\nC) 原词陷阱\nHe is starting a business. 盗用了原文 I think his family owns the business 里的\nbusiness，但原文说的是\"他家族拥有这门生意（早就在做）\"，不是\"他正在创业\n（starting）\"，把\"已有生意\"听成\"开始创业\"就中招。\n借用原文：his family owns the business\nD) 过度推断\nHe is a successful car dealer. 顺着\"家里开车行、懂车\"往下推出\"成功的车商\"，但原文只\n说他 knows a lot about cars、人好，从没评价生意\"成功（successful）\"，这是从已有信\n息多走了一步的过度推断。\n借用原文：he knows a lot about cars", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn about early pigs in North America?", "options": ["A) Many escaped from farms and became wild.", "B) They were actually native to North America.", "C) Many got killed in the wild when searching for food.", "D) They were hunted by Spanish and Russian explorers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Many escaped from farms and became wild.\n【定位】This practice also allowed many pigs to escape from farms and live in the wild, which\nbecame a problem.\n这种做法也使得许多猪从农场逃走、生活在野外，这就成了一个问题。\n【信号】讲来历的开头句。本段是交代猪\"从哪来\"的起源段，承载答案的是收束这段经过的 This\npractice also allowed many pigs to escape from farms and live in the wild——它正面\n说出了\"许多猪逃离农场进入野外\"这一结果，是答案句。\n【替换】many pigs to escape from farms and live in the wild ↔\nMany escaped from farms and became wild\nlive in the wild=became wild\n【排除】B)\n原文开篇即说 Pigs are not native to North America（猪并非北美土生），而 They were\nactually native to North America 恰恰说成\"本来就是北美土生\"，极性反了。\n借用原文：Pigs are not native to North America\nC) 未提及\n原文只说猪 escape from farms and live in the wild（逃走进野外），从未提到\"许多猪在\n野外觅食时被杀\"，Many got killed in the wild when searching for food 是无中生有，\n只是借用了原文 in search of food 的影子。\nD)\n原文确有 Spanish and Russian explorers and settlers，但说的是他们\"introduced（引\n入）\"了猪，They were hunted by Spanish and Russian explorers 却把动作改\n成\"hunted（猎杀）\"，盗了专名、换了行为，是答非所问。\n借用原文：introduced to California by Spanish and Russian explorers and settlers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Why are wild pigs a threat to humans?", "options": ["A) They often make sudden attacks on people.", "B) They break up nature's food supply chain.", "C) They cause much environmental pollution.", "D) They carry a great many diseases."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They carry a great many diseases.\n【定位】They also harbor dozens of diseases that threaten both humans and farm animals.\n它们还携带数十种威胁人类和家畜的疾病。\n【信号】唯一含humans的句子。全段讲危害的句子很多，但点名\"threaten...humans\"的只有这一\n句 harbor dozens of diseases that threaten both humans and farm animals，题眼\"对\n人\"直接对上它，是答案句。\n【替换】harbor dozens of diseases that threaten both humans ↔ They carry a great many diseases\nharbor=carry、dozens=many\n【排除】A)\n原文从未提野猪\"主动攻击人\"，只说携带疾病威胁人；They often make sudden attacks\non people 是凭\"野猪凶猛\"的常识脑补，原文无据。\nB) 答非所问\n原文确实提到野猪扰乱食物链类的生态危害（eating other animals or their food\nsupply），但那是对其他动物/生态的危害，不是题眼问的\"对人\"；They break up nature's\nfood supply chain 盗用了原文真讲过的危害，却答非所问。\n借用原文：In addition to either eating other animals or their food supply\nC)\n原文说野猪 rooting up grasses and rubbing on trees（拱草、蹭树）破坏原生栖息地，\n这是物理破坏栖息地，不是\"环境污染\"；They cause much environmental pollution 偷换\n成了 pollution，且仍不是\"对人\"。\n借用原文：damage native habitats by rooting up grasses and rubbing on trees", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the passage say about the native foxes on the US island?", "options": ["A) They lived peacefully with wild pigs.", "B) They ran out of food completely.", "C) They fell victim to eagles.", "D) They reproduced quickly."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They fell victim to eagles.\n【定位】The eagles began breeding on the island, and also feeding on a species of native fox. The\nfoxes were almost wiped out completely.\n老鹰开始在岛上繁殖，也开始捕食一种本地狐狸。这些狐狸几乎被彻底消灭。\n【信号】狐狸出现处的因果链。本段结尾交代了\"野猪招来老鹰→老鹰捕食狐狸→狐狸几乎灭绝\"的因\n果链，承载答案的是 feeding on a species of native fox 与 The foxes were almost\nwiped out completely 这两句，直接说出狐狸的下场，是答案句。\n【替换】feeding on a species of native fox ↔ They fell victim to eagles\n被老鹰捕食=成老鹰的猎物\n【排除】A)\n原文说狐狸几乎被消灭，谈不上\"和平共处\"；They lived peacefully with wild pigs 借用了\n原文反复出现的 wild pigs，却把惨烈结局说成\"和平相处\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：The foxes were almost wiped out completely\nB)\n原文谈\"food supply\"是说野猪抢夺动物的食物来源，并未说狐狸\"彻底断粮\"；They ran\nout of food completely 盗用了原文的 food supply 与 completely，张冠李戴到狐狸\"断\n粮\"上，实际狐狸是被老鹰吃掉。\n借用原文：eating other animals or their food supply\nD)\n原文说 The eagles began breeding on the island（老鹰开始在岛上繁殖），They\nreproduced quickly 把\"繁殖\"这个动作从老鹰头上挪到了狐狸头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The eagles began breeding on the island", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What are a pair of entrepreneurs planning to do?", "options": ["A) Taste coffee while in outer space.", "B) Roast coffee beans in outer space.", "C) Develop a new strain of coffee bean.", "D) Use a pressurised tank to brew coffee."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Roast coffee beans in outer space.\n【定位】A pair of entrepreneurs are planning to build and launch a spacecraft that would carry and\nroast coffee beans in outer space.\n两位创业者正计划建造并发射一艘能在外太空运载并烘焙咖啡豆的飞行器。\n【信号】原文 planning to 与题干同词，是直接应答信号。录音第一句就用 are planning to 直接对\n上题干的 planning to do，这种题干问什么、原文紧接着用同一表计划的词说出来的句\n子，就是答案句，顺着这个信号锁定即可。\n【替换】roast coffee beans in outer space ↔ Roast coffee beans in outer space.\n原文短语与选项几乎逐词对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nTaste coffee while in outer space. 借了后文做咖啡、卖咖啡的话题诱你联想到品尝，但原\n文这两人计划的是 roast 烘焙，从没说要在太空 taste 品尝咖啡，属答非所问。\nC) 未提及\nDevelop a new strain of coffee bean. 说培育咖啡豆新品种，原文通篇只讲烘焙现有的豆\n子、从未提培育新品种，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nUse a pressurised tank to brew coffee. 盗用了原文 in a pressurised tank 这个真出现过\n的原词来骗你，但原文那个加压罐是用来 roast 烘焙豆子的，不是 brew 冲泡咖啡，听到熟\n词就选正中圈套。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the passage say about coffee beans roasted on Earth?", "options": ["A) They can easily get burned.", "B) They float around in the oven.", "C) They have to be heated to 360℃.", "D) They receive evenly distributed heat."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They can easily get burned.\n【定位】The businessmen say that on Earth, beans can easily break apart and get burned in the roaster.\n这些商人说，在地球上，咖啡豆在烘焙机里容易裂开并被烧焦。\n【信号】原文 on Earth 与题干限定词重合，是定位锚。录音 on Earth, beans can easily break\napart and get burned 里的 on Earth 与题干限定词完全一致，听到这个限定词就锁\n定本句；其后 But if gravity is removed 一转，转折后讲的是太空情形，不可拿来答\non Earth。\n【替换】get burned in the roaster ↔ They can easily get burned.\n原文与选项共用 get burned，连 easily 都对上\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nThey float around in the oven. 盗用原文 the beans float around in a heated oven 这\n句真念过的话，可那是 But if gravity is removed 之后讲的太空情形，题问的是 on Earth\n地球，张冠李戴。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey have to be heated to 360℃. 盗用原文 360 degrees of evenly distributed heat 里\n的 360 这个真念过的数字，可原文 360 degrees 指的是太空里受热的方位360度而非加热\n到360℃，且讲的也是太空情形，数字陷阱加错情形双重出错。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey receive evenly distributed heat. 几乎照搬原文 evenly distributed heat，可这是 if\ngravity is removed 之后描述太空里受热均匀的好处，题问的是 on Earth 地球情形，把太\n空的优点安到地球头上，张冠李戴。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What did the two Italian companies do in 2015?", "options": ["A) They charged a high price for their space-roasted coffee beans.", "B) They set up a branch in Dubai to manufacture coffee roasters.", "C) They collaborated on building the first space coffee machine.", "D) They abandoned the attempt to roast coffee beans in space."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They collaborated on building the first space coffee machine.\n【定位】In 2015, two Italian companies collaborated on the construction of a similar type of\nspacecraft, which was the first coffee machine designed for use in space.\n2015年，两家意大利公司合作建造了一艘类似的飞行器，那是首台为太空使用而设计的咖\n啡机。\n【信号】原文 In 2015 与题干年份重合，是定位锚。录音 In 2015, two Italian companies\ncollaborated on the construction 同时出现 In 2015 与 two Italian companies 两个题干\n锚，听到它们就锁定本句，本句的主动作 collaborated 即答案。\n【替换】collaborated on the construction of a similar type of spacecraft ↔\nThey collaborated on building the first space coffee machine.\nconstruction 概括为 building，意思等值\nthe first coffee machine designed for use in space ↔ the first space coffee machine\n原文长定语压缩成选项短语，所指相同\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey charged a high price for their space-roasted coffee beans. 盗用前文 how much\nthey would charge 关于收费的话，可那是讲前文那对创业者卖咖啡定价、且只说不清收多\n少，根本不是2015年两家意大利公司做的事，张冠李戴。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey set up a branch in Dubai to manufacture coffee roasters. 盗用前文 sold for the\nfirst time in Dubai 里真念过的 Dubai，可 Dubai 只是前文那对创业者公司的所在地，原\n文从没说意大利公司去 Dubai 设分部造烘焙机，属无中生有加张冠李戴。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey abandoned the attempt to roast coffee beans in space. 盗用原文 the first\nattempt to take coffee into space 里的 attempt，可原文是说2015年并非首次尝试、那\n两家公司是合作建成了机器，根本没说放弃，方向相反。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "Why do a lot of people come to the village of Takotna every March?", "options": ["A) It is the best time for sightseeing.", "B) A race passes through it annually.", "C) They come to clean the Iditarod Trail.", "D) It is when the villagers choose a queen."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A race passes through it annually.\n【定位】Each March, this tiny village swells up in numbers, because it is located in the middle of a\nrace that takes place every year.\n每年三月，这个小村庄人数都会激增，因为它正位于一场每年举办的比赛的途中。\n【信号】因果连词 because 引出真因。这句的答案信号是因果连词 because：题干问 \"为什么三月\n人多\"，录音正好用 because it is located in the middle of a race that takes place every\nyear 给出原因——听到 because 就该锁定其后内容为答案句。\n【替换】a race that takes place every year ↔ A race passes through it annually\ntakes place every year = annually\nit is located in the middle of a race ↔ A race passes through it\n村在赛道中 = 赛事穿村而过\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt is the best time for sightseeing. 说三月是观光最佳时节，但录音只讲了比赛带来人流，\n全程没提观光或景色好坏，属凭常识脑补出来的理由。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey come to clean the Iditarod Trail. 偷用了录音里真出现过的赛事名 Iditarod Trail 来\n骗 \"听到就想选\"，但录音说人们是来参赛/被吸引，根本没说来 \"打扫\"（clean）这条小径，\n盗了名字却安了个录音没有的动作。\n借用原文：It is a seven-day race, called the Iditarod Trail\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt is when the villagers choose a queen. 借了录音末尾 started to refer to Jan as\nQueen of Takotna（村民称 Jan 为 \"塔科特纳王后\"）里的 queen 一词，但那只是对一个人\n的尊称，并非 \"三月选王后\" 的活动，更不是人们来访的原因，把后文的称呼挪来当来访理\n由。\n借用原文：Proud residents then started to refer to Jan as Queen of Takotna", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is the village of Takotna famous for?", "options": ["A) Its children's baking skills.", "B) Its unique winter scenery.", "C) Its tasty fruit pies.", "D) Its great food variety."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Its tasty fruit pies.\n【定位】Lucky for them, Takotna is famous for its delicious fruit pies.\n对他们来说幸运的是，塔科特纳以美味的水果派而闻名。\n【信号】标志短语 famous for 直接点题。答案信号就是题干同款短语 famous for：录音原话\nTakotna is famous for its delicious fruit pies 与题干 famous for 用词几乎对照，听到\nfamous for 就锁定其后的 fruit pies 为答案。\n【替换】its delicious fruit pies ↔ Its tasty fruit pies\ndelicious = tasty 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIts children's baking skills. 搬用了录音里真出现的 developing their baking skills（孩子\n们练就烘焙手艺），但录音说孩子练手艺只是全村备战的一个细节，村子 \"出名\" 的点录音\n明确说是水果派，不是孩子的手艺——盗了 baking skills 却答非所问。\n借用原文：who end up developing their baking skills at an early age\nB) 未提及\nIts unique winter scenery. 说村子以独特冬景闻名，录音开头确实提到 cold and snowy\nAlaska（寒冷多雪的阿拉斯加）这一背景，但从没说村子靠 \"冬景\" 出名，是把 \"地处雪国\"\n脑补成了 \"以雪景著称\"。\n借用原文：In cold and snowy Alaska\nD) 偷换概念\nIts great food variety. 借了第二段 pies of all kinds, such as apple, orange, lemon or\nbanana（各种口味的派）的 \"多样\" 印象，但那是在讲招待racers的派有多种口味，而村子\n\"出名\" 的点录音说的是 fruit pies 这一招牌本身，不是 \"食物种类丰富\"——把口味多偷换成\n了出名理由。\n借用原文：delightful pies of all kinds, such as apple, orange, lemon or banana", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Who comes to help with the event of the year?", "options": ["A) The contestants.", "B) The entire village.", "C) Jan Newton and her friends.", "D) People from the state of Idaho."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The entire village.\n【定位】The whole village chips in to help, including the kids, who end up developing their baking\nskills at an early age.\n整个村子都来帮忙，连孩子们也不例外，他们因此从小就练就了烘焙手艺。\n【信号】范围词 whole 锁定参与主体。答案信号是表范围的 whole 加动词短语 chips in to help：\n录音 The whole village chips in to help 直接点出帮忙的是 \"整个村子\"，听到 whole\nvillage… help 就锁定它为答案句。\n【替换】The whole village chips in to help ↔ The entire village\nwhole village = entire village\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nThe contestants. 指参赛选手，录音里他们是 The exhausted and hungry racers are\ngreeted with delightful pies（又累又饿、被村民用派招待）的一方，是被帮的人，不是来\n帮忙的人，方向正好弄反。\n借用原文：The exhausted and hungry racers are greeted with delightful pies of all kinds\nC) 原词陷阱\nJan Newton and her friends. 借用了第三段才出现的人名 Jan Newton 来骗选，但 Jan 是\n后文讲 \"村子出名缘起\" 的人物，且录音从没说 \"Jan 和她的朋友们\" 来帮忙办活动——答案\n句明明是 \"全村\"，这一项把范围错误地缩成了少数人。\n借用原文：It started with one person, Jan Newton\nD) 原词陷阱\nPeople from the state of Idaho. 偷用了录音里真出现的地名 Idaho（Jan moved from\nIdaho 指 Jan 当年从 Idaho 搬来），但录音只说 Jan 一人来自 Idaho，从没说 \"一群爱达\n荷人\" 来帮忙办活动，纯属盗用地名拼凑出的人群。\n借用原文：Jan moved from Idaho with her husband in 1972", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the passage say about Jan Newton?", "options": ["A) She owned a restaurant in Idaho.", "B) She married her husband in 1972.", "C) She went to Alaska to compete in a race.", "D) She helped the village to become famous."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She helped the village to become famous.\n【定位】Her rich and filling-fruit pies quickly got the racers' attention, and the village gained some\nfame as a result.\n她那香浓饱腹的水果派很快吸引了参赛选手的注意，村子也因此小有名气。\n【信号】结果信号 as a result 点出她的贡献。答案信号是结果状语 as a result：录音说她的派吸引\n选手注意，the village gained some fame as a result（村子因此出了名）——这处因果\n落点正对应 \"Jan 让村子出名\"，是核对四项后唯一与原文相符的陈述。\n【替换】the village gained some fame as a result ↔ She helped the village to become famous\n因她而村子出名 = 她帮村子出名\ngained some fame ↔ become famous\ngain fame = become famous\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nShe owned a restaurant in Idaho. 偷用了录音里真出现的地名 Idaho，但录音说 Jan 是\nmoved from Idaho（从 Idaho 搬出来）后才 opened a restaurant，餐馆其实是开在\nTakotna，不是 Idaho——盗了地名却把餐馆地点安错了。\n借用原文：Jan moved from Idaho with her husband in 1972 and opened a restaurant\nB) 原词陷阱\nShe married her husband in 1972. 偷用了录音真出现的年份 1972，但录音 Jan moved\nfrom Idaho with her husband in 1972 说的是 1972 年 \"她带着丈夫搬来\"（搬家），并没\n说 1972 年 \"结婚\"——而且 with her husband 说明搬来时已是丈夫，把搬家的年份硬安给\n了结婚。\n借用原文：Jan moved from Idaho with her husband in 1972\nC) 张冠李戴\nShe went to Alaska to compete in a race. 借了通篇反复出现的 race（比赛）一词，但录\n音说 Jan 是搬来开餐馆、做派招待选手，从没说她 \"去阿拉斯加参赛\"——去参赛的是\nracers，不是 Jan，把比赛这条线张冠李戴到了她头上。\n借用原文：Her rich and filling-fruit pies quickly got the racers' attention", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) crawling\n动词\n【词性槽】逗号后补充说明主语动作，缺现在分词\n【依据】蚂蚁「爬出」地巢钻进烈日\ncrawling「爬行」描述银蚁 from their underground nests into the sun's brutal rays 从\n地下巢穴爬向烈日的动作，符合蚂蚁的行进方式与全文觅食情境。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) hunt\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to 后，缺动词原形\n【依据】to hunt for food = 觅食，正合主旨\n全文开头点明银蚁外出 to seek lunch，hunt 与 for food 构成 hunt for「搜寻、觅食」，\n与「这是觅食好时机」完全一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) extreme\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 heat 前作定语，缺形容词\n【依据】形容沙漠「极端」高温最贴\n全文反复渲染 120 degrees Fahrenheit 的酷热，extreme「极端的、极度的」修饰 heat\n表「极端高温」，与主旨完全契合。\n【竞争词】H) moderate 义反，温和与酷热相悖\nmoderate「适度的、温和的」恰与沙漠 120 华氏度的极热相反。\nM) thick thick 不修饰 heat\nthick「厚的」不与 heat 搭配，且应留给 __(31)__ 的毛发。\nI) remote 偏远修饰不了热量\nremote「偏远的」用于地点而非热度，语义不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) species\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 后作宾语，缺名词\n【依据】指银蚁这一「物种」如何适应酷热\n全文研究对象是 the Saharan silver ant 这种蚂蚁，species「物种」指代这一生物种类如何\nadapted to 高温，与主旨吻合。\n【竞争词】L) specimens 标本是死物，不会主动「适应」\nspecimens「标本、样本」指采集的实物样品，没有生命，不能作 adapted to「适应」的主\n语；这里讲的是活体物种如何适应酷热。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— specimens 与 species 形近、都搭科研语境，最易错选。\n两词同为名词、都能跟在 the 后，且「标本」与科学家研究高度相关，但 the __(29)__\nadapted to the kind of heat 的谓语是「适应」，只有活物种能适应，死标本不能；防\n坑：看谁能当这个谓语的主语。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) literally\n副词\n【词性槽】助动词 can 与实义动词 melt 之间，缺副词\n【依据】强调「真能把鞋底融化」非夸张\ncan __(30)__ melt the bottom of shoes 在渲染高温之极，literally「确确实实地、简直」\n强调这是字面意义上真会发生，强化酷热程度。\n【竞争词】B) consciously 高温无意识可言，主语错位\nconsciously「有意识地」用于有思维的主体，而这里主语是 heat（热量），热不会「有意识\n地」融鞋，语义荒谬。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— consciously 词性对、位置也能放，易当副词随手填。\nconsciously 与 literally 同为副词、都能塞进 can __(30)__ melt the bottom of shoes，但\n主语是 heat（热量），热不会「有意识地」融鞋；防坑：副词必须和被修饰动词的施动者\n语义相容，无意识主体排除 consciously。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) thick\n形容词\n【词性槽】与 triangular 并列修饰 hair，缺形容词\n【依据】描述毛发「浓密/粗」，与三角形、反光相称\n下文说这种毛 reflects light like a prism 像棱镜反光，thick「厚的、浓密的、粗的」描述\n毛发的物理形态，与三角形截面、强反光相配。\n【竞争词】H) moderate 「适度的毛发」无意义\nmoderate「适度的」修饰 hair 不传递任何形态信息，不贴。\nI) remote 偏远修饰不了毛发\nremote「偏远的」与 hair 完全不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) removed\n动词\n【词性槽】从句主语 Willot 后缺谓语动词（过去式）\n【依据】「去掉/移除」蚂蚁毛发再加热做实验\n后文 put it under a heat lamp, its temperature jumped 是把取下的毛发拿去加热，\nremoved「移除、去掉」正描述「从蚂蚁身上取下毛发」这一动作。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) tiny\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 knife 之间，缺形容词作定语\n【依据】蚂蚁毛发极细，配「微小的」刀刃才合理\n上文 Quentin Willot __(32)__ the hair from an ant 是从一只蚂蚁身上取毛发，这等精细\n操作要用极小的刀，tiny「极小的」最贴。\n【竞争词】M) thick 义反，厚刀切不下蚂蚁毛\nthick「厚的、粗的」与精细取毛的语境相反，且 thick 已为 __(31)__ 描述毛发留着，更不该填\n这里。\nE) extreme 搭配不通，无 extreme knife 之说\nextreme「极端的」不修饰刀具，且要留给 __(28)__/__(30)__ 处的酷热语境。\nH) moderate 语义空泛，与精细取毛无关\nmoderate「适度的」修饰刀毫无信息，不贴语境。\nI) remote 义不搭，偏远修饰不了刀\nremote「偏远的」用于地点，配 knife 不成立。\nO) unique 义不搭，且要留给 __(34)__\nunique「独特的」修饰刀无意义，且该词是 __(34)__ 处「方法独特」的最佳人选。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) unique\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 is 后作表语，缺形容词\n【依据】反光毛降温在动物中「独特」，引出可否用于人类\n下文紧接 Could this reflective type of hair protect people? 用反问凸显其稀有可贵，\nunique「独一无二的」正点出该方法在动物界少见、值得借鉴。\n【竞争词】M) thick 厚不能修饰「方法」\nthick「厚的」修饰 method 不通，且要留给 __(31)__ 的毛发。\nE) extreme 极端的方法语义偏，且留给酷热\nextreme「极端的」不贴「独特罕见」的语气，且是 __(28)__/__(30)__ 酷热语境的强候选。\nH) moderate 「适度的方法」无信息且不引出后文\nmoderate「适度的」既空泛，又无法支撑下句「能否用于人类」的转折。\nI) remote 义不搭，偏远修饰不了方法\nremote「偏远的」与 method 语义不相关。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adapting", "B) consciously", "C) crawling", "D) crowded", "E) extreme", "F) hunt", "G) literally", "H) moderate", "I) remote", "J) removed", "K) species", "L) specimens", "M) thick", "N) tiny", "O) unique"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adapting\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 in 后接动名词，需 -ing 动词\n【依据】企业有意「改造」银蚁防热法供人类用\n下文 these ants' method of heat protection for human use 是把蚂蚁方法改用于人，\nadapting「改造、使适用」正对这层意思。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "The number of people experiencing depression shows a sharp increase in the first year of high school.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】抑郁人数在高中第一年陡增\n【定位】studies find the first year of high school typically shows one of the greatest increases in\ndepression of any year over the lifespan\n研究发现，高中第一年通常呈现出人一生中抑郁增幅最大的年份之一。\n【改写】The number of people experiencing depression ↔ increases in depression\n经历抑郁的人数等价于抑郁的升幅\nsharp increase ↔ one of the greatest increases\n急剧升高=史上最大升幅之一\nthe first year of high school ↔ the first year of high school\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 A A段的关键描述是a dramatic drop in a student's academic performance，与抑郁\n完全不同；它只与题干的入学时间点相邻，并未提抑郁。\nnearly two-thirds of students will experience the \"ninth-grade shock,\" which refers to a dramatic\ndrop in a student's academic performance", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to one study, students' academic performance is not the only decisive factor of their stress responses.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学业表现不是压力反应的唯一决定因素\n【定位】This was an exciting result because it showed that the body's stress responses are not\ndetermined solely by one's grades.\n这是一个令人振奋的结果，因为它表明身体的压力反应并非仅仅由成绩决定。\n【改写】students' academic performance ↔ one's grades\n学业表现=成绩\nis not the only decisive factor ↔ are not determined solely by\n并非唯一决定因素=并非仅被…决定\nstress responses ↔ the body's stress responses\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 I I段的核心是Students who got the growth mindset intervention带来更好的生理反\n应，未提是否仅由成绩决定。\nStudents who got the growth mindset intervention(干预) showed less-contracted blood vessels and\ntheir hearts pumped more blood", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Researchers would like to explore further how parents and schools can help ninth graders by changing their mindset.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】研究者想进一步探索家长与学校如何通过改变思维模式帮助九年级生\n【定位】We also hope to learn how teachers, parents or school counselors can help students keep their\nongoing academic or social difficulties in perspective.\n我们还希望了解教师、家长或学校辅导员如何帮助学生以恰当的视角看待他们持续存在的学\n业或社交困难。\n【改写】Researchers would like to explore further ↔\nThese findings lead to several possibilities that we are investigating further\n想进一步探索=正在进一步研究\nparents and schools ↔ teachers, parents or school counselors\n学校=学校里的教师与辅导员\nninth graders ↔ students starting the ninth grade\n九年级生=刚进入九年级的学生\nchanging their mindset ↔ beliefs about the potential for change and improvement\n改变思维模式=关于改变之潜力的信念\n【辨邻】 F F段说的是当前研究迄今得到的主要洞见our studies have yielded one main\ninsight，并未提及未来要做的事。\nour studies have yielded one main insight: Students' beliefs about change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "According to one study, each high school graduate contributes at least 500,000 dollars to the local economy.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】每名高中毕业生至少为当地经济贡献五十万美元\n【定位】One study has calculated that the lifetime benefit to the local economy for a single additional\nstudent who completes high school is half a million dollars or more.\n一项研究算出，每多一名读完高中的学生，给当地经济带来的终身效益是五十万美元或\n更多。\n【改写】each high school graduate ↔ a single additional student who completes high school\n每一名毕业生=每多一名读完高中的学生\ncontributes at least 500,000 dollars ↔\nthe lifetime benefit to the local economy ... is half a million dollars or more\n至少五十万美元=五十万美元或更多\nthe local economy ↔ the local economy\n原样复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "In one study, students were told their social position in school is not unchangeable.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】被告知其在校的社交地位并非不可改变\n【定位】we taught them that their social standing—that is, whether they are bullied or excluded or left\nout—can change over time\n我们告诉他们，他们的社交地位——也就是说，他们是否被霸凌、被排斥或被冷落——是可\n以随时间改变的。\n【改写】students were told ↔ we taught them\n被告知=我们教他们\ntheir social position in school ↔ their social standing\n校内社交位置=社交地位\nis not unchangeable ↔ can change over time\n并非不可改变=可以随时间改变\n【辨邻】 F F段讨论的是whether people can change their personalities and abilities，并未提\n及social standing或bully/exclude等具体社交内容。\na \"growth mindset\" referring to the belief that people can change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is reported that depression results in enormous economic losses worldwide.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】抑郁造成全球巨大经济损失\n【定位】The World Health Organization reports that depression has the greatest burden of disease\nworldwide, in terms of the total cost of treatment and the loss of productivity.\n世界卫生组织报告称，抑郁是全球疾病负担最重的，体现在治疗的总成本和生产力的损\n失上。\n【改写】It is reported ↔ The World Health Organization reports\n被动据报道=具名机构的reports\ndepression results in enormous economic losses ↔\ndepression has the greatest burden of disease worldwide, in terms of the total cost of treatment\nand the loss of productivity\n巨大经济损失=治疗成本+生产力损失这一负担\nworldwide ↔ worldwide\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 C C段讨论the lifetime benefit to the local economy，是单个学生对地方经济的正向\n贡献，与全球性的经济损失方向相反。\nthe lifetime benefit to the local economy for a single additional student who completes high school\nis half a million dollars or more", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One study showed that friendships among ninth graders were far from stable.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】九年级生友谊远谈不上稳定\n【定位】One study of ninth-grade students found that 50 percent of friendships among ninth graders\nchanged from one month to the next, signaling striking instability in friendships.\n一项针对九年级学生的研究发现，九年级生之间百分之五十的友谊在前后两个月间发生了变\n化，显示出友谊明显的不稳定。\n【改写】friendships among ninth graders ↔ friendships among ninth graders\n原样复现\nfar from stable ↔ striking instability in friendships\n绝非稳定=显著不稳定", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "More than half of students will find their academic performance declining sharply when they enter the ninth grade.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】过半数学生进入九年级后学业表现急剧下滑\n【定位】Consider that nearly two-thirds of students will experience the \"ninth-grade shock,\" which\nrefers to a dramatic drop in a student's academic performance.\n想想看：将近三分之二的学生会经历所谓的九年级冲击——指学生学业表现的急剧下滑。\n【改写】More than half of students ↔ nearly two-thirds of students\n过半=接近三分之二\ntheir academic performance declining sharply ↔\na dramatic drop in a student's academic performance\n学业表现急剧下滑=学业表现显著下降\nwhen they enter the ninth grade ↔ experience the \"ninth-grade shock\"\n进入九年级即九年级冲击发生时点\n【辨邻】 G G段聚焦Students who believed that intelligence is fixed这一部分学生的反应，未\n给出涵盖整体的过半数比例。\nStudents who believed that intelligence is fixed—that you are stuck being \"not smart\" if you\nstruggle in school—showed higher levels of stress hormones when their grades were declining at\nthe beginning of the ninth grade", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Researchers found through experiments that students could be taught to respond to stress in a more positive way.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】实验发现学生可被教导以更积极方式应对压力\n【定位】But when students were taught that people can change, they had better responses to stress, in\npart because they felt like they had the resources to deal with the demanding situation.\n但是当学生被教导人可以改变时，他们对压力的反应变得更好，部分原因在于他们感到自己\n拥有应对挑战情境的资源。\n【改写】through experiments ↔ Experiment results showed that\n通过实验=实验结果显示\nstudents could be taught ↔ when students were taught\n可以被教导=被教导这一假设条件\nrespond to stress in a more positive way ↔ they had better responses to stress\n更积极方式应对压力=对压力的反应更好\n【辨邻】 H H段交代的是研究流程：students came into the laboratory and were asked to\ngive a public speech，并未陈述更好的压力反应这一结果。\nwe taught them a growth mindset about their social lives\nG G段考察的是学生原本就持有的信念差异下的反应，关键句是If students believed\nthat intelligence could improve，并未实施教导干预。\nIf students believed that intelligence could improve—that is to say, when they held more of a\ngrowth mindset of intelligence—they showed lower levels of stress hormones when their grades\nwere declining", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "It is beneficial to explore ways to cope with the challenges facing students entering high school.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】探索如何应对高中新生面对的挑战是有益的\n【定位】Given all that's riding on having a successful ninth grade experience, it pays to explore\nwhat can be done to meet the academic, social and emotional challenges of the transition to\nhigh school.\n鉴于成功度过九年级这件事关乎甚多，探索能采取哪些措施以应对升入高中所带来的学业、\n社交和情感挑战是值得的。\n【改写】It is beneficial to explore ways ↔ it pays to explore what can be done\n值得探索=有益地探索可做什么\ncope with the challenges ↔ meet the academic, social and emotional challenges\n应对挑战=直面学业社交情感挑战\nstudents entering high school ↔ the transition to high school\n进入高中的学生=向高中过渡这一过程的主体\n【辨邻】 B B段的核心表态是This should matter a great deal to parents, teachers and\npolicymakers，关注点在该不该被重视，而非探索方法的有益性。\nThis should matter a great deal to parents, teachers and policymakers\nJ J段说的是研究者下一步要做什么、想知道什么，未给出beneficial这种价值判断；其表\n述如We want to know in which types of schools都属于具体计划。\nWe want to know in which types of schools and for which kinds of students these growth mindset\nideas help young people adapt to the challenges of high school", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say about educators?", "options": ["A) They seek advice from technology companies to achieve teaching goals.", "B) They have been successful in preparing the workforce for companies.", "C) They help students acquire the skills needed for their future success.", "D) They partner with technology companies to enhance teaching efficiency."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about educators 问的是作者对教育者说了什么客观陈\n述，没有 infer/imply/mainly about，是定位 + 转述型细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author say about educators 主干是 \"the author say what about\neducators\" 的疑问倒装，about 后面跟话题对象，结构简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】Teachers want to prepare students for a successful future.\n教师想让学生为成功的未来做好准备。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey seek advice from technology companies to achieve teaching goals 说教师向\n科技公司寻求建议，原文只说双方有共同点 Educators and business leaders have\nmore in common than it may seem，并没有任何「教师向公司讨教」的方向性陈述。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey have been successful in preparing the workforce for companies 用现在完成\n时说教师已经成功地把劳动力交付给公司，原文只说 Teachers want to prepare\nstudents for a successful future——是 want to 的愿望，不是已实现的成果。\nC) ✓ 正确\nTeachers want to prepare students for a successful future ↔\nThey help students acquire the skills needed for their future success\n预判要求「准备 + 未来 + 学生」三要素齐全，They help students acquire the skills\nneeded for their future success 把三个要素一一对应，无新增也无遗漏，是定位句的\n标准同义替换。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey partner with technology companies to enhance teaching efficiency 说教师与\n公司合作以提升教学效率，原文虽然提到 technology companies are partnering with\neducators to bring back good, old-fashioned play，但目的是把游戏带回课堂，不是\n「提升教学效率」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How can educators better develop students' STEM skills, according to the author?", "options": ["A) By blending them with traditional, stimulating activities.", "B) By inviting business leaders to help design curriculums.", "C) By enhancing students' ability to think in a critical way.", "D) By showing students the best way to learn is through play."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How can educators better develop students' STEM skills 问的是具体方法，需要回\n原文找一句明确的「办法」陈述，属于方法型细节题。\n【拆句】How can educators better develop students' STEM skills, according to the author：\n主干 educators develop STEM skills，better 是副词修饰 develop，according to the\nauthor 是插入语，整句问的是方法，结构简单。\n【定位】Focusing on STEM skills is important, but the reality is that STEM skills are enhanced and\nmore relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative activities.\n关注 STEM 技能很重要，但事实是，只有当 STEM 技能与传统的、动手的创造性活动相结\n合时，这些技能才能得到强化，也才更具相关性。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nSTEM skills are enhanced and more relevant when combined with traditional, hands-on creative\nactivities\n↔ By blending them with traditional, stimulating activities\n预判的三要素「结合 + 传统 + 动手/创意」，By blending them with traditional,\nstimulating activities 命中「结合」「传统」两项硬词，「动手创意」被合理上位化为\nstimulating，整体语义等值。\nB) 无中生有\nBy inviting business leaders to help design curriculums 说请商业领袖来帮忙设计课\n程，原文只说商业领袖与教师有共同利益、且科技公司与教师合作把游戏带回来\ntechnology companies are partnering with educators to bring back good, old-\nfashioned play，没有任何「商业领袖设计课程」的说法。\nC) 偷换概念\nBy enhancing students' ability to think in a critical way 把方法说成「增强批判性思\n维」，但原文里 the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific\ntechnologies or subject matter, but to creativity 列出的是 creativity、\nimagination、problem-solving，并没有 critical thinking 这一项。\nD) 过度推断\nBy showing students the best way to learn is through play 说告诉学生学习的最佳\n方式是玩，原文虽然反复肯定 play 的价值 Play may be the answer，但用 may 表推\n测，且没有把 play 表述为 the best way to learn，属于对原文力度的放大。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "How do children acquire the skills needed for the 21st century?", "options": ["A) By engaging in activities involving specific technologies.", "B) By playing with things to solve problems on their own.", "C) By familiarizing themselves with high-tech gadgets.", "D) By mastering basic principles through teamwork."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How do children acquire the skills needed for the 21st century 是 How + 一般疑\n问，问获得方式，定位 → 同义替换即可，不需要主旨级归纳。\n【拆句】How do children acquire the skills needed for the 21st century：主干 children\nacquire the skills，needed for the 21st century 是过去分词后置定语修饰 skills，等价于\nthe skills which are needed for the 21st century。\n【定位】These are skills acquired when kids tinker.\n这些（21 世纪所需的）能力，正是孩子们在鼓捣小玩意时获得的。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nBy engaging in activities involving specific technologies 说通过涉及具体技术的活\n动来获得能力，但 the most important 21st-century skills aren't related to specific\ntechnologies or subject matter, but to creativity 明确说不是关联于 specific\ntechnologies，选项与原文方向相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThese are skills acquired when kids tinker ↔\nBy playing with things to solve problems on their own\n预判核心「自己动手 + 鼓捣 / 玩耍」在 By playing with things to solve problems on\ntheir own 中以 playing with things + on their own 完整复现，并把要获得的能力具\n体化为 solve problems，与同段的 problem-solving 完全一致。\nC) 与原文相反\nBy familiarizing themselves with high-tech gadgets 说让孩子熟悉高科技装置，但 it\ndoesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills 明确说培养这些\n技能并不需要 21 世纪的高科技。\nD) 张冠李戴\nBy mastering basic principles through teamwork 把方式说成「通过团队合作掌握基\n本原理」，原文同段列出的能力清单 skills like imagination, problem-finding and\nproblem-solving, teamwork, optimism, patience and the ability to experiment\nand take risks 里虽然出现 teamwork，但 teamwork 是要获得的能力之一，不是获得\n能力的途径，且 basic principles 出现在第四段说的是数学几何，与 21 世纪技能不是同\n一回事。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What can we do to help children learn the basics of math and geometry?", "options": ["A) Stimulate their interest as early as possible.", "B) Spend more time playing games with them.", "C) Encourage them to make things with hands.", "D) Allow them to tinker freely with calculators."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What can we do to help children learn the basics of math and geometry 问的是\n建议性做法，回原文找一句明确的做法陈述即可，属于方法型细节题。\n【拆句】What can we do to help children learn the basics of math and geometry：主干 we\ndo what，to help 是目的状语，help children learn 是 help sb. do 结构，learn the\nbasics of math and geometry 是 help 的宾补，无难点。\n【定位】This type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key\nprinciples in math and geometry.\n这种游戏——尤其是搭积木——能帮助孩子发现并掌握数学和几何中的关键原理。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nStimulate their interest as early as possible 说要尽早激发兴趣，原文虽提到 four-\nand five-year-olds Research shows that given 15 minutes of free play, four- and\nfive-year-olds will spend a third of this time engaged in spatial, mathematical,\nand architectural activities，但并没有提出「激发兴趣」这一做法，更没有「越早越\n好」的表态。\nB) 偷换概念\nSpend more time playing games with them 说大人花更多时间陪孩子玩游戏，原文\n的关键是「孩子自己搭建」 This type of play—especially with building blocks—\nhelps children discover and develop key principles in math and geometry，并未\n要求大人陪玩，更没说 more time 这一程度。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThis type of play—especially with building blocks—helps children discover and develop key\nprinciples in math and geometry\n↔ Encourage them to make things with hands\n预判核心「动手 + 搭建/制作」，Encourage them to make things with hands 同时\n命中 make（建造/制作）与 with hands（动手），无新增信息，是 building blocks 的\n合理上位概括。\nD) 与原文相反\nAllow them to tinker freely with calculators 把鼓捣的对象换成计算器，原文却强调\nbuilding blocks 这类纯动手物件；且 it doesn't take 21st-century technology to\nfoster 21st-century skills 已经否定了高科技工具的必要性，calculators 属于反向。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author advise disadvantaged schools and communities to do?", "options": ["A) Train students to be makers to meet future market demands.", "B) Develop students' creative skills with the resources available.", "C) Engage students with challenging tasks to foster their creativity.", "D) Work together with companies to improve their teaching facilities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author advise disadvantaged schools and communities to do 是\n定向问作者建议，需要找一句明确针对弱势学校的建议句，是定位型细节题，不需要全篇\n归纳。\n【拆句】What does the author advise disadvantaged schools and communities to do：主干\nthe author advise sb. to do sth.，advise sb. to do 是固定搭配，disadvantaged ...\ncommunities 整体作 advise 的宾语，结构清晰。\n【定位】This is especially important for under-resourced schools and communities. Taking whatever\nmaterials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those important\n\"maker\" skills.\n这一点对于资源匮乏的学校和社区尤为重要。拿手边任何能找到的材料动手鼓捣，就是激发\n这些重要「制造者」能力的一种简单办法。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTrain students to be makers to meet future market demands 把目的设为「满足未\n来市场需求」，原文里反复说的是培养 maker / creator 等能力 Children are born\nbuilders, makers, and creators，从未出现 market demands 这类商业目的。\nB) ✓ 正确\nTaking whatever materials are handy and tinkering with them is a simple way to engage those\nimportant \"maker\" skills\n↔ Develop students' creative skills with the resources available\nit doesn't take 21st-century technology to foster 21st-century skills ↔\nDevelop students' creative skills with the resources available\n预判的「现有/手边资源 + 创造性能力」，Develop students' creative skills with the\nresources available 同时命中 with the resources available（现有资源）与 creative\nskills（创造性能力），两项语义点齐全且力度匹配。\nC) 与原文相反\nEngage students with challenging tasks to foster their creativity 说让学生做有挑\n战性的任务，原文却强调 fostering 21st-century skills may be as simple as giving\nkids room to play, tinker and try things out，用的是 as simple as，方向是「越简单\n越好」。\nD) 张冠李戴\nWork together with companies to improve their teaching facilities 说与公司合作改\n善教学设施，原文虽然在首段提到 Educators and business leaders have more in\ncommon than it may seem，但末段给资源匮乏地区的建议只是用手边材料鼓捣，从\n未提到「与公司合作改善设施」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about working in IT?", "options": ["A) It is envied by many.", "B) It does not appeal to him.", "C) It is financially rewarding.", "D) It does not match his abilities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about working in IT? 用 what 引导，问作者对某一具体对象\n的明确表态，属于带主观色彩的细节题，而不是 infer/imply。\n【拆句】题干是简单疑问句，主干为 What does the author say about working in IT?，about 引\n出话题，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Being an information technology, or IT, worker is not a job I envy.\n成为一名信息技术（即 IT）工作者，不是一份我会羡慕的工作。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is envied by many. 把作者 not a job I envy 的态度改成\"很多人羡慕\"，方向完全相\n反，且原文也没说\"很多人羡慕\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnot a job I envy ↔ does not appeal to him\n预判\"作者不羡慕 IT 工作\"与 It does not appeal to him. 同义；其余选项与作者表态相\n反或与文意无关。\nC) 无中生有\nIt is financially rewarding. 称 IT 工作\"经济回报丰厚\"，但全文从未谈及薪资或金钱收\n益，纯属编造。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt does not match his abilities. 说 IT 工作\"与他的能力不匹配\"，原文只说作者不羡\n慕、并描述 IT 工作辛苦，从未涉及作者本人的能力是否够格。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the finding of a recent survey on employees who have a job reliant on IT support?", "options": ["A) IT helps boost productivity.", "B) IT helps improve quality control.", "C) Many employees are deeply frustrated by IT.", "D) Most employees rely heavily on IT in their work."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding of a recent survey 直接问一项调查的发现，是对原文具体信息的复\n述，属于细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What is the finding of a recent survey on employees，on 引出调查对象、who 从\n句进一步限定。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】According to a recent survey, employees who have a job reliant on IT support consider IT a\nmajor source of job dissatisfaction.\n根据一项最新调查，工作依赖 IT 支持的员工把 IT 视为工作不满的主要来源。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIT helps boost productivity. 说 IT 提升生产力，与原文 their productivity\ndeteriorating（生产力下降）完全相反。\nB) 与原文相反\nIT helps improve quality control. 说 IT 改善质量控制，但原文是 quality control\nnon-existent，质控\"不复存在\"，方向反转。\nC) ✓ 正确\nconsider IT a major source of job dissatisfaction ↔\nMany employees are deeply frustrated by IT\n预判\"员工对 IT 极度不满\"与 Many employees are deeply frustrated by IT. 完全吻\n合；其他三项要么把 IT 说成正面作用，要么扯到使用频率，均偏离结论。\nD) 答非所问\nMost employees rely heavily on IT in their work. 说\"大多数员工严重依赖 IT\"，但题\n干问的是调查的 finding，而原文 finding 是\"员工不满\"，不是\"员工依赖\"；\"依赖\"只是\n调查对象的限定条件，不是调查结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is said to happen when IT is functioning properly?", "options": ["A) There is a big boost in employees' work efficiency.", "B) Employees become more dependent on machines.", "C) There are no longer any boring or repetitive tasks.", "D) Employees become more confident in their work."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is said to happen when IT is functioning properly? 问在某条件下会发生什么具体\n结果，是原文已有陈述的复述题，属细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is said to happen，when 从句修饰 happen，is said 是被动语态，弱化\n作者主观色彩，照常按客观细节处理。\n【定位】When IT is operating as it should, employee self-confidence swells.\n当 IT 像它本应那样运转时，员工的自信心便会膨胀。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThere is a big boost in employees' work efficiency. 把答案换成\"工作效率大幅提\n升\"，原文只说自信和工作满意度上升，并未在 IT 正常运转时给出\"效率大幅提升\"的结\n论；\"a big boost\"也属于原文未表达的程度夸大。\nB) 偷换概念\nEmployees become more dependent on machines. 说员工\"更依赖机器\"，原文谈的\n是 IT 正常时员工\"自信增强\"，未涉及依赖加深；\"依赖加深\"通常是负面评价，与本段 IT\n正常时的正向描述也不符。\nC) 过度推断\n原文 well-functioning machines relieve them of dull tasks or repetitive processes\n说机器\"减轻\"枯燥重复任务，There are no longer any boring or repetitive tasks. 却\n说\"不再有任何\"重复任务，把\"缓解\"夸大为\"彻底消除\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nemployee self-confidence swells ↔ Employees become more confident in their work\n预判\"员工自信增强\"与 Employees become more confident in their work. 几乎逐字\n同义；其余选项不是过度推断就是与原文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What should business leaders do before implementing new IT initiatives?", "options": ["A) Consider the various expectations of their customers.", "B) Draw up a list of the efficiencies to be promised.", "C) Assess the swollen cost of training the employees.", "D) Think about the possible effects on their employees."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What should business leaders do before implementing new IT initiatives? 问\"应当做\n什么\"，对应原文里直接给出建议/批评的句子，是细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What should business leaders do，before 引导时间状语 before implementing\nnew IT initiatives，implementing 是动名词作介词宾语。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Which is why managers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to\ntheir employees, and exert substantial effort in ensuring their IT team eliminates\nprogramming errors and application crashes.\n正因如此，管理者应当意识到 IT 用户体验对员工有多大影响，并下大力气确保 IT 团队消除\n程序错误与应用崩溃。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nConsider the various expectations of their customers. 把考虑对象从员工换成\"客\n户\"，但全文从未谈及 customers，关注点始终是 employees/end user。\nB) 与原文相反\nDraw up a list of the efficiencies to be promised. 把\"承诺的效率\"列成清单，原文恰\n恰批评 a long list of promised efficiencies has been reversed，即\"承诺过的效率被\n打回原形\"，是失败之处而非应做之事。\nC) 无中生有\nAssess the swollen cost of training the employees. 借用 swollen expectations\nhave been popped 中的 swollen 拼出\"激增的培训成本\"，但原文 swollen 形容的是\nexpectations（被吹胀的预期），从未提培训成本。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwith little consideration of how those changes will impact the end user ↔\nThink about the possible effects on their employees\nmanagers should appreciate just how influential the IT user experience is to their employees ↔\nThink about the possible effects on their employees\n预判\"先考虑对员工的影响\"和 Think about the possible effects on their employees.\n高度吻合；A/B/C 要么换错对象，要么偏离核心动作。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How can a business help improve its employees' experience in using IT?", "options": ["A) By designing systems that suit their needs.", "B) By ensuring that their mental health is sound.", "C) By adjusting their work to suit the IT system.", "D) By offering them regular in-service training."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How can a business help improve its employees' experience in using IT? 问公司具体\n如何改善，对应原文给出的具体做法句，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 How can a business help improve its employees' experience，in using IT 是后置\n介词短语修饰 experience。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Therefore, businesses need to set up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit\nin with their employees' work, rather than adjust their work to fit in with the company's\nIT limitations.\n因此，企业需要这样搭建其 IT 基础设施：使其设计去适配员工的工作，而不是让员工调整\n工作去迁就公司的 IT 局限。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nset up their IT infrastructure so that it is designed to fit in with their employees' work ↔\nBy designing systems that suit their needs\n预判\"IT 去适配员工\"严丝合缝对应 By designing systems that suit their needs.；\nB/C/D 要么扯心理健康、要么取了 rather than 后的反向做法、要么属于无中生有的\n培训。\nB) 偷换概念\nBy ensuring that their mental health is sound. 借用 what employees experience\nmentally when they use IT 里的 mentally 拼出\"确保心理健康\"，但原文是\"理解员工使\n用 IT 时的心理体验\"，不是\"维护员工心理健康\"，且这一点也不是末段给出的总对策。\nC) 与原文相反\nBy adjusting their work to suit the IT system. 把 rather than 后的反面做法 adjust\ntheir work to fit in with the company's IT limitations 当成对策，方向完全反转。\nD) 无中生有\nBy offering them regular in-service training. 提到\"定期在职培训\"，全文从未谈及培\n训这件事，纯属新概念。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
